"""r18's check texts in the romance article (2026-11-07), each linted before its check (gate bad-trips-2026-11-07h).
- "What are you actually choosing together?", its second and last section try: r17's text with S20's three Low questions
  (#103) fixed: the short opener joined to the first question with a colon, the either/or question left a little
  unbalanced ("Are we promising actions (or low-key pretending ...)?"), and the rethink question joined to the split-up
  one, its kids in brackets.
- Three sections with a spot in them, whole, heading left out, each with the spot's best try and its last Low line fixed:
  "The conversation about flaws" (122 to 128, S7 as #34 read, its closing question joined to the one before with a colon
  and his "okay" kept), "From flow to nitty gritty" (199 to 201, S9's line said once at the end of the bed paragraph
  instead of as a summary paragraph after it) and "Which marriage vows are honest?" (349 to 352, S21's closing said first,
  in the verse sentence: the verses "make the wedding vow itself look even more suspicious (to me, anyway)", "even
  more" carrying what "stranger still" did, the step past Ecclesiastes).
- "What I gained from loss" (443 to 456), whole, with 443's short line joined to the Rumi line it goes with, its only Low
  window in the last part's check (#47)."""
TEXTS = {
 "r18-choosing": "A lot of romantic pain starts with two lovey bunnies entering one of the biggest arrangements of their lives without ever discussing their ideas, dreams, and fears about it.\n\n“We’re together” ... what does that mean exactly? I can hear a whole future in those two words—sex, exclusivity, living together, money, children, caregiving. She might mean, “We really like each other; let’s see what happens.” I actually like letting a relationship flow with some parts left unnamed, but the problem is when we’re using the same words for two different futures.\n\nSometimes what two people want is much more practical. They want to share a home, raise children, support each other financially, take care of each other, maybe have sex, and not pretend all of that depends on overwhelming romantic love. I’d call that a practical partnership. There’s nothing inherently wrong with that arrangement if both people know that’s what they’re doing. But I suspect they simply have never been in love, so they don’t have that expectation. And that becomes a problem if they ever do fall for someone.\n\nAnother honest option is a romance with humility, even if romance and humility seem to clash. Two people really are in love and want to build something together, and they can choose exclusivity, shack up, have children, and plan a life, as long as nobody pretends they can guarantee what either heart will feel ten years later.\n\nThen the questions change for me: if the romance changes, which parts are still supposed to remain? Are we promising actions (or low-key pretending to promise permanent feelings)? Which needs are we still going to meet outside the relationship?\n\nAnd the biggie: what happens if one of us falls in love with someone else? What counts as betrayal, and what’s just a sign we need a serious talk? If our lives change, can we honestly sit down and rethink the agreement, and if we split up, what do we still owe each other (and our kids, if we have any)?\n\nThese are just a few questions I could think of to start the ball rolling, but be creative and see what you come up with! You can make a card game out of this and have some fun with it. If there’s real love, it will survive a bit of honest curiosity. And again, community can make all of this a lot less scary.",
 "r18-convo": "Card games and social gatherings don’t tell you everything, but I wouldn’t ask someone out of the blue, “What did you do wrong in your past relationships?” That would be a pretty weird date.\n\nAt the right time, though, in the right way (especially if they bring it up tangentially), I would ask about their past relationships, especially the recent ones. If every story ends with everything being the other person’s fault, I’d ask, “Okay, so what lessons did you find for yourself?”\n\n“I have baggage” tells me almost nothing. What are you having trouble with now? What might make it hard for you to function well in a relationship?\n\nI’d want both of us to be honest about how effective we actually are at handling our own issues. What addictions or impulsive behaviors can’t we reliably control? How dependable are we? What are we probably going to ask forgiveness for more than once? That tells me more than either of us announcing that we’re healed.\n\nThe most recent conflicts are the best place to look. Were you able to regulate at all during the conflict? What did you do to the other person? How did you repair afterward? What did you learn? Has your judgment gotten better, or do you keep falling for the same stuff? What would your friends or exes say?\n\nAnd if you tell me you shut down, okay, what does that mean: did you stop talking to your partner for days, and did they feel abandoned?",
 "r18-nitty": "Living alone is easy in so many ways. I can make my bed whenever I want. When I have a girlfriend, making the bed in the morning can become one of the ways I show her I love her. If I don’t do it well, somehow it may become a bad omen that I don’t care about her, which is a lot of emotional weight for a bed.\n\nDealing with somebody else’s emotional insecurities can also be exhausting. It’s easy to feel free when I don’t have to do that, especially since I’m a bit of an introvert and 10% autistic. All that works wonderfully until I’m in a romantic relationship and suddenly I’m expected to mind-read, notice every emotional shift, check in constantly, and understand what a silence means, without manipulating based on all that expert “management.”",
 "r18-vows": "Conventional marriage vows are dishonest, and most people know it. You can’t promise how you’ll feel in the future. You can promise actions if you really believe you’re capable of delivering them. So for example, you could promise to tell the truth, to keep showing up, to support the children, or whatever else you genuinely believe you can do.\n\nWe also find something like my warning in the New & Old Testaments. Ecclesiastes 5:4–6 says it is better not to make a vow than to make one and fail to keep it. If you tell the priest you didn’t mean what you promised, the passage warns that God will be angry and destroy your work.\n\nMatthew 5:33–37 and James 5:12 make the wedding vow itself look even more suspicious (to me, anyway): let your “Yes” be “Yes” and your “No” be “No,” and anything beyond that comes from evil.\n\nWhere did the idea of promising to have romantic interest in your partner come from, anyway? It turns out it never existed in any culture I’m aware of until the 19th century, when people in England began citing romance as their reason for marriage. That trend has continued evolving like a snowball until today. But what our ancestors knew was not wrong: You can sign a contract about what you’ll do, but feelings aren’t contractual.",
 "r18-loss": "At this point, I can say I’m better off because of what happened with Bee, Key, and H., without pretending every part of those relationships was beneficial or destined. Loss taught me things love alone never would, or to paraphrase Rumi, “You must keep breaking your heart until it stays open.” (The real quote: “The reed must be torn from its bed, cut, hollowed out, and pierced with holes (wounded) before it can channel the breath of the musician into music.”)\n\nFrom Bee, I learned what it means for me to be really in love. I learned the kind of woman I actually want: deeply spiritual, sensitive, creative, unique, somebody who inspires me and makes me question everything.\n\nKey repeated a lot of that, which is why I fell in love with her. I learned some of the same harsh lessons from both of them: don’t take openness and curiosity to be fully good. They can also indicate that someone hasn’t developed their own sense of self or opinions, or enough inner adult.\n\nFrom H., I learned that I can be extremely close to someone and feel completely understood and supported without actually falling in love. I still treasure the support we gave/give each other.\n\nToo many people judge a relationship only by the result, so start measuring what you actually learned from it instead. Relationships are a process of growth in many dimensions. The result is not only whether you stayed together.\n\nRomantic love is not easy, and the things that actually make it work are not very marketable. I can’t sell you the fix with one click or one monthly subscription to my love class. (Just kidding, click here!)\n\nA relationship like this is rare, and that’s probably why romantic love has been seen with suspicion as a destabilizing force throughout history. One of the best books that makes that point today might be the one I was reading with Key: The Spirit of Intimacy, by Sobonfu Somé. Somé came from the Dagara tribe in Burkina Faso. To her people, marriage is an arranged, ritualized spiritual and communal responsibility. They’re suspicious of romance. But one of her ideas fits what I’m trying to say: the relationship is bigger and more important than the egos in it. She calls it the spirit of the relationship.\n\nThat spirit is like the universe living its desire to know itself through us, which might be why we see ourselves in ways we can’t imagine prior. Nobody can command the spirit to remain. At our best, we serve it and listen for what Spirit wants to change. When we start thinking we own it or deserve it, that presumptuousness tends to push it away.\n\nMy parents agreed with Somé that community matters and that we serve Spirit. They were also not romantic, though they were loving and poly. I actually wished they were in love with each other, I guess, although I couldn’t have articulated that since I didn’t even have that concept at the time. Also reminiscent of Somé, they also wore rose-colored glasses and taught me that everybody was basically good. So I learned something about love and community, but not how hard romance is without a community around it, or how much damage basically good people can do when their fears, trauma, and undeveloped parts take over.\n\nIt took me many decades to figure out what probably everyone else already knows: real private pathology exists. But pathology festers when the environment is perfect for it. The dominant culture hermetically seals two people together and calls that a luxurious privacy. It tells them that sealed room should provide all the fresh air you’d get from a lover, family, community, therapist, and so on. Then it treats the predictable failure as privately caused as well, like mold that grows out of nowhere.\n\nThe myths around romantic love become real because we co-create them. We act as though jealousy proves love, permanent feelings can be promised, conflict should stay hidden, and the relationship failed if it ended. Then we suffer inside ideas that were never natural laws, and we walk blindly into our first, second, third, fourth, and fifth marriages!\n\nIt might be that I wrote this whole article for my son, Bear, who sadly, I last saw when he was about five, right before he was supposed to get the Rosenblum sex talk.\n\nBear, sex can be what you do when you’re older and find a friend you love in a super special way that makes you want to raise children together. Don’t imagine you love each other just because you want each other. Don’t feel pressure to promise what your heart will feel. Let the love and bond it produces blossom honestly as your hearts sync. Learn about each other before getting too close, and make sure to keep friends and community around who know both of you.\n\nI believe Rumi was right: A sacred relationship will open and purify your hearts regardless of whether it ends."
}
